{"description":"Publishing from a registered repository attributes SOME types to the repository and others to the workspace sentinel 'runtime'. Observed in one batch on eka-cli: td-/ts- work items, ctr- and plan- landed with source_repo 'eka-cli'; tkt- and cmt- (created minutes later from the same working directory, through the same authoring API) landed with source_repo 'runtime'. sync push filters by source_repo, so the notes and tickets never enter the repository snapshot while the work items they describe do.","impact":"The evidence trail does not travel. The cmt- that satisfies the R13 implementation gate, and the tkt- that registers container membership (registeredInActiveContainer reads tkt- units deriving from the active ctr- and the work item), exist only in the originating workspace database. A clone that pulls the repository snapshot sees finished work items whose notes and tickets are missing, so the gate and the membership rules read as violated there and cannot be satisfied locally. The wave-5 notes in the snapshot exist only because a capture reconcile from the docs tree put them there, which is not part of the publish path.","acceptanceCriteria":"- One rule, stated and enforced: either every type published from a repository context is repository-attributed, or the types that are deliberately workspace-native are listed with the reason, and publish says which case it took.\n- `eka publish` from a repository context names the attribution it chose, so a note that will never reach the snapshot is visible at authoring time instead of at clone time.\n- A test asserts the attribution of a note and a ticket published inside a registered repository, so the two paths cannot drift apart silently."}